WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.050 line:90%
Want to remove something from your database?

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.388 line:90%
These two commands can leave
you with very different results.

3
00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:07.962 line:90%
DELETE… and DROP.

4
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.924 line:90%
One of them keeps your
table. The other one doesn't.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.756 line:90%
Let's see which is which.

6
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.434 line:90%
Here's a small Employees table.

7
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:21.500 line:90%
First, CREATE TABLE builds the structure:
three columns — id, name, and department.

8
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.744 line:90%
Think of it as an empty
cabinet with labeled slots.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.052 line:90%
Then INSERT adds the rows: four
employees, two of them in Sales.

10
00:00:29.302 --> 00:00:34.062 line:90%
Two separate things, then: the table's
structure, and the rows stored inside it.

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.961 line:90%
Let's remove only the Sales employees.

12
00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:40.578 line:90%
DELETE FROM Employees, WHERE
department equals 'Sales'.

13
00:00:40.828 --> 00:00:42.616 line:90%
The WHERE clause is the filter.

14
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.415 line:90%
The database checks each row's department…

15
00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:47.546 line:90%
and marks the ones that match.

16
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.409 line:90%
So which rows will go?

17
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.696 line:90%
Just these two.

18
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.393 line:90%
The Sales rows are gone —

19
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:58.627 line:90%
but Priya and Marcus remain, along
with the headers and the table itself.

20
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.189 line:90%
Selected rows removed. Table stays.

21
00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:09.043 line:90%
Let's reset to the original four rows.

22
00:01:09.293 --> 00:01:12.695 line:90%
Now the same DELETE — but
notice: no WHERE clause.

23
00:01:12.944 --> 00:01:15.080 line:90%
No filter means every row matches.

24
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:18.031 line:90%
Run it… and all four rows are removed.

25
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.228 line:90%
But look: the Employees table is still here.

26
00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:26.181 line:90%
Its name, its columns, its
structure — all intact. Just empty.

27
00:01:26.431 --> 00:01:32.642 line:90%
So before running destructive SQL, check: is there
a WHERE clause, and does it match what you intend?

28
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.738 line:90%
One more reset, back to four rows.

29
00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:38.345 line:90%
This time: DROP TABLE Employees.

30
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.993 line:90%
Watch what happens.

31
00:01:40.243 --> 00:01:41.243 line:90%
The rows go…

32
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.500 line:90%
the headers go…

33
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.498 line:90%
the table itself is gone.

34
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.018 line:90%
There is no Employees table anymore.

35
00:01:47.268 --> 00:01:51.726 line:90%
Back to our cabinet: DELETE removes
the contents, and the cabinet stays.

36
00:01:51.976 --> 00:01:54.101 line:90%
DROP removes the cabinet itself.

37
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:55.400 line:90%
Quick quiz.

38
00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:59.132 line:90%
Remove only employees
in Sales. Which command?

39
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.672 line:90%
DELETE, with a WHERE clause.

40
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.811 line:90%
Rows filtered, table kept.

41
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.138 line:90%
Next: remove the Employees
table itself. Which command?

42
00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.300 line:90%
DROP TABLE.

43
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.086 line:90%
The whole table is gone.

44
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.489 line:90%
So remember: DELETE removes
rows, and the table remains.

45
00:02:21.739 --> 00:02:24.165 line:90%
DROP TABLE removes the table entirely.

46
00:02:24.416 --> 00:02:27.644 line:90%
DELETE clears the contents.
DROP removes the container.
